00:17:19,531 --> 00:17:23,950
I guess I never expected things with
a man of your caliber to- to be normal.

241
00:17:25,700 --> 00:17:28,942
- I- I gotta confess, I've never actually
read anything you've written.

242
00:17:28,942 --> 00:17:33,048
- Uh, tell me, Doctor, what exactly
is your position at the gallery?

243
00:17:35,483 --> 00:17:37,825
I sit in the corner...

244
00:17:39,072 --> 00:17:40,329
and look at the paintings.

245
00:17:40,329 --> 00:17:41,904
[EXCLAIMS]

246
00:17:41,904 --> 00:17:43,344
That is brilliant!

247
00:17:44,071 --> 00:17:47,927
- If only more scholars would
do that, you know, just sit and look.

248
00:17:47,927 --> 00:17:50,523
- You know, not lecturing,
write and argue.

249
00:17:50,547 --> 00:17:53,267
Just sit and look at the
paintings themselves.

250
00:17:53,603 --> 00:17:55,851
Now that is brilliant. Yep.

251
00:18:00,220 --> 00:18:01,437
Yes.

252
00:18:02,318 --> 00:18:04,939
- Morning, Elmer.
- Good morning, sir.

253
00:18:04,963 --> 00:18:06,919
- Dr Bean?
- Oh.

254
00:18:07,742 --> 00:18:09,874
- Would you like
to sign in, sir?

255
00:18:15,320 --> 00:18:18,112
Thank you. Welcome to
the Grierson, Dr Bean.

256
00:18:23,939 --> 00:18:25,755
Welcome to my world.

257
00:18:30,676 --> 00:18:33,743
We're expected in Grierson's
office in 9am, now this is a man

258
00:18:33,743 --> 00:18:35,924
who does not like to be
kept waiting, so if you--

259
00:18:35,924 --> 00:18:38,052
- Do you mind if I--
- Oh, yeah, okay, absolutely.

260
00:18:38,052 --> 00:18:39,837
You bet.

261
00:18:39,861 --> 00:18:41,605
All right.

262
00:18:46,510 --> 00:18:48,100
[ZIPS PANTS]

263
00:18:48,124 --> 00:18:50,559
[SIGHS CONTENTLY]

264
00:18:51,528 --> 00:18:53,512
[HUMMING HAPPILY]

265
00:19:02,324 --> 00:19:03,762
[EXCLAIMS]

266
00:19:04,478 --> 00:19:06,298
- [GASPING]

267
00:19:22,731 --> 00:19:24,594
[DRYER HUMS]
[GASPS]

268
00:19:28,388 --> 00:19:31,761
[HOPPING GRUNTS]

269
00:19:40,946 --> 00:19:44,418
[SIGHING HAPPILY]

270
00:20:14,296 --> 00:20:15,730
Hi.

271
00:20:22,243 --> 00:20:25,613
[HMM-ING]

272
00:20:29,090 --> 00:20:30,889
[GASPING IN PAIN]

273
00:20:31,628 --> 00:20:33,902
[BULB SHATTERS]
[EXCLAIMS]

274
00:20:34,348 --> 00:20:37,307
Doctor? We should make a
move. We're very late.

275
00:20:39,951 --> 00:20:41,969
[GASPING IN PAIN]

276
00:20:44,511 --> 00:20:45,783
[EXCLAIMS]

277
00:20:55,578 --> 00:20:57,720
Oh! we-- you--

278
00:20:57,744 --> 00:21:00,654
Doctor... [STUTTERS]
When you're ready.

279
00:21:00,822 --> 00:21:02,022
Okay.

280
00:21:07,547 --> 00:21:09,230
- Ah, David, finally.

281
00:21:12,200 --> 00:21:14,419
And this must be our
professor from across the sea.

282
00:21:14,443 --> 00:21:16,433
Yes. George Grierson,
this is Dr Bean.

283
00:21:16,457 --> 00:21:17,963
I'm delighted, Doctor.

284
00:21:19,112 --> 00:21:21,530
- Actually, I'm no--
- Dr Bean! Can we get a photo, please?

285
00:21:21,570 --> 00:21:22,986
[CAMERA SHUTTERS]
Right there.

286
00:21:22,986 --> 00:21:24,753
We've heard a great deal
about you, Doctor.

287
00:21:25,327 --> 00:21:27,191
[WHISPERING] We expect
a great deal of you.

288
00:21:28,918 --> 00:21:30,853
- And this is Bernice,
in charge of PR.

289
00:21:30,877 --> 00:21:33,352
- An absolute honor, Doctor.
- all right, thank you.

290
00:21:33,352 --> 00:21:35,917
And Walter Huntley,
our merchandise manager.

291
00:21:37,265 --> 00:21:40,648
- Nice to meet you...
- Moving along. Take a seat, Doctor.

292
00:21:41,289 --> 00:21:43,805
- Here, let me take that old thing.
- [EXCLAIMS]

293
00:21:43,829 --> 00:21:45,782
This is very out of date.

294
00:21:46,320 --> 00:21:47,462
- Thank you.

295
00:21:47,486 --> 00:21:51,243
- Before we settle in, you might
want to feast your eyes on these.

296
00:21:58,747 --> 00:21:59,961
Oh--

297
00:22:01,551 --> 00:22:03,477
[CHUCKLES]

298
00:22:04,517 --> 00:22:06,308
Well, I'll be honest
with you, Bean.

299
00:22:06,308 --> 00:22:08,529
- I like this.
- It's beautiful, sir.

300
00:22:09,554 --> 00:22:11,006
- It is. Yeah.
- Mmm!

301
00:22:12,550 --> 00:22:14,128
- Over here.
- Okay.

302
00:22:25,446 --> 00:22:27,226
Lovely green...

303
00:22:28,284 --> 00:22:30,905
- Good, good. Down to business.
- Okay...

304
00:22:31,948 --> 00:22:35,533
- I've asked Bernice to run through
the order of events for the opening of...

305
00:22:35,533 --> 00:22:37,832
Dr Bean, would you
like to join us?

306
00:22:38,236 --> 00:22:41,322
[MUTTERING]
Uh, no-- no, I'll-

307
00:22:42,901 --> 00:22:44,456
- Very well...

308
00:22:44,752 --> 00:22:46,102
Bernice.

309
00:22:46,539 --> 00:22:49,989
Now, you will know that anything
pertaining to special media requirements

310
00:22:49,989 --> 00:22:52,313
- is prefixed with an asterisk,
- Mm-hmm.

311
00:22:52,337 --> 00:22:57,123
- i.e., glossy stills requested by some
of the favored weeklies. That sort of thing.

312
00:22:57,147 --> 00:23:01,540
- Right. So, the big day
unfolds as follows:

313
00:23:01,540 --> 00:23:03,971
- Our generous benefactor,
General Newton, will be

314
00:23:03,971 --> 00:23:06,345
arriving at 10am to meet
the members of the staff.

315
00:23:06,345 --> 00:23:09,422
- Then at 10:30, we'll have
the unveiling of the picture.

316
00:23:09,446 --> 00:23:12,081
- Now, this will be in front
of all press and media.

317
00:23:12,105 --> 00:23:15,779
Walter, I'm assuming all this merchandising
is gonna be made available.

318
00:23:15,779 --> 00:23:18,986
Oh, absolutely. From fridge magnets
to frizbees, we'll be ready.

319
00:23:19,010 --> 00:23:24,958
- So it will probably be at about 11am
when the stage is set for Dr Bean.

320
00:23:24,982 --> 00:23:27,833
- [MOANING]
- Yes. I really...

321
00:23:27,858 --> 00:23:30,289
really should bring in
the Doctor, here.

322
00:23:30,974 --> 00:23:34,087
[MOANING]

323
00:23:39,322 --> 00:23:40,606
Uh, Dr Bean?

324
00:23:44,795 --> 00:23:47,222
[ GRUNTS IN CONTENT]

325
00:23:51,860 --> 00:23:54,971
- Doctor, we've just been
inspecting this schedule.

326
00:23:54,995 --> 00:23:56,270
and--

327
00:23:56,270 --> 00:23:59,921
If you would just like to take a look
at yours over there at your convenience...

328
00:23:59,945 --> 00:24:02,431
Uh, the General should
be arriving at 10am,

329
00:24:02,431 --> 00:24:05,074
- and he'll unveil the
picture at 10:30.

330
00:24:05,098 --> 00:24:08,893
So at about 11:00 we should
be, um, ready for your speech.

331
00:24:10,824 --> 00:24:14,221
- Yes, and uh, I think...
twenty minutes should...

332
00:24:14,221 --> 00:24:15,820
should be long enough.

333
00:24:15,844 --> 00:24:17,884
- Don't you, Bernice?
- Absolutely.

334
00:24:17,908 --> 00:24:21,001
I know you could fill hours,
Doctor, but for thew press,

335
00:24:21,001 --> 00:24:23,410
- it would be best if you
could keep it quite light.

336
00:24:23,434 --> 00:24:26,731
Maybe throw in some anecdotes
about actual painting of the picture,

337
00:24:26,755 --> 00:24:30,670
uh, and explain why it is considered
to be America's greatest painting.

338
00:24:30,670 --> 00:24:35,472
- [EXCLAIMS QUIETLY]
- Is that going to be enough, Doctor?

339
00:24:35,472 --> 00:24:36,774
Twenty minutes?

340
00:24:37,938 --> 00:24:40,309
[MUTTERS]

341
00:24:40,537 --> 00:24:43,960
Well, dear friends, if you'll excuse
me, I have a lunch with the Mayor.

342
00:24:43,984 --> 00:24:47,173
Not the kind of thing
I like, but duty calls.

343
00:24:47,197 --> 00:24:49,433
Well, Doctor, it's
good to meet you.

344
00:24:49,766 --> 00:24:51,574
It's a complex experience.

345
00:24:52,785 --> 00:24:57,033
Enjoy your stay with David.
They're simple people but warm.

346
00:24:57,308 --> 00:25:01,149
- David, if I may have just a private word?
- Okay. Uh...

347
00:25:01,173 --> 00:25:03,588
I tell you, why don't you wait for
me back at the new acquisitions.

348
00:25:03,612 --> 00:25:05,565
I'll join you shortly. Yeah.

349
00:25:12,556 --> 00:25:16,917
- He's a genius, right?
- Yeah, he's eccentric, but...

350
00:25:16,941 --> 00:25:18,727
- Yes.
- Yeah.

351
00:25:21,889 --> 00:25:25,809
The unveiling of Whistler's Mother
is the most important thing

352
00:25:25,833 --> 00:25:31,299
to ever happen to this gallery,
and Dr Bean was your choice.

353
00:25:31,775 --> 00:25:34,046
Yes, I understand that.

354
00:25:34,894 --> 00:25:36,483
Thank you, sir.

355
00:25:52,338 --> 00:25:54,708
Dr Bean, when we
get home, I wonder--

356
00:25:54,732 --> 00:25:56,091
[SIGHS]

357
00:25:56,115 --> 00:26:00,944
When we get home, I wonder if you would
mind giving me a private moment with my wife.

358
00:26:01,362 --> 00:26:03,761
There's a little something
we need to work out.

359
00:26:03,785 --> 00:26:05,017
No problem.

360
00:26:13,311 --> 00:26:14,848
[MIXER WHIRRING]

361
00:26:15,418 --> 00:26:18,074
Yeah, I think, uh... Just wait
in here would be good.

362
00:26:19,108 --> 00:26:22,744
Oh, just ignore that.
Her mother gave it to us.

363
00:26:22,768 --> 00:26:25,369
- Huge sentimental
value, you know, so...

364
00:26:26,117 --> 00:26:29,590
- Just make yourself at home. I'll be
just a couple of minutes with Alison.

365
00:26:35,775 --> 00:26:39,746
- Oh! Doctor, I would
actually not do that.

366
00:26:39,770 --> 00:26:44,026
- That's... that's a limited edition.
It's that it's delicate, so if you...

367
00:26:44,050 --> 00:26:47,420
- Or, yeah, if... Put it there
would be good. Yeah.

368
00:26:47,444 --> 00:26:49,169
Okay, that's... All right.

369
00:26:49,193 --> 00:26:52,027
[MIXER WHIRRING]

370
00:26:54,984 --> 00:26:56,962
- Hi.
- Hi!

371
00:26:58,213 --> 00:27:01,053
- No Dr Bean?
- Well actually, no, he is still here,

372
00:27:01,077 --> 00:27:03,985
- but let me-- I just want us to--
- Jennifer! Kevin!

373
00:27:04,339 --> 00:27:06,446
- Plan B!
- What is-- You have luggage packed?

374
00:27:06,470 --> 00:27:08,885
- What is Plan B?
- Plan B is,

375
00:27:08,909 --> 00:27:11,963
if you haven't gotten rid of Dr Bean,
we go straight to Grandma's.

376
00:27:11,987 --> 00:27:14,031
- I can't believe you--
- Screwed up again, huh, Dad?

377
00:27:14,055 --> 00:27:16,594
Oh, come on! There was no
time to raise the subject!

378
00:27:16,618 --> 00:27:20,540
- Kevin, start the car, would you, sweetie?
- Oh, Ali, wait a second and look.

379
00:27:20,564 --> 00:27:25,083
No, you look, David, my darling.
I have asked very little from you,

380
00:27:25,107 --> 00:27:28,742
but I did ask you to get rid of Dr Bean,
and you have made your choice,

381
00:27:28,766 --> 00:27:33,005
so you two can stay here and really
come to grips with postmodernism,

382
00:27:33,029 --> 00:27:35,866
but I am taking our
children to my mother's.

383
00:27:35,890 --> 00:27:38,203
Just give him one last chance.

384
00:27:38,227 --> 00:27:41,001
- Oh, David...
- Just one.

385
00:27:41,411 --> 00:27:42,867
Please?

386
00:27:44,975 --> 00:27:46,443
Okay.

387
00:27:46,831 --> 00:27:49,681
- Okay. All right.
- Thank you. Thanks.

388
00:27:49,937 --> 00:27:51,325
Once you get to know him,

389
00:27:51,349 --> 00:27:53,214
- I think you're gonna--
- David, don't.

390
00:27:58,989 --> 00:28:01,004
- Hello, Doctor.
- Hello.

391
00:28:01,396 --> 00:28:03,375
[BEEPING]

392
00:28:04,130 --> 00:28:06,436
- No, no, no, no, Doctor, that
is not for the TV. That's for the...

393
00:28:06,460 --> 00:28:10,483
[ALISON GASPING]
[GLASS SHATTERING]

394
00:28:21,515 --> 00:28:22,898
Yeah.

395
00:28:24,081 --> 00:28:25,516
Well.

396
00:28:26,265 --> 00:28:27,487
There they go.

397
00:28:34,599 --> 00:28:37,764
Well, Doctor, we've got
the whole weekend together.

398
00:28:38,920 --> 00:28:42,955
Yeah, get to know
each other and, uh...

399
00:28:44,630 --> 00:28:47,135
I was thinking tomorrow
we might really do LA.

400
00:28:47,159 --> 00:28:51,756
Uh, there's the Gallery of American Indian
Art. There's the California State Gallery.

401
00:28:51,780 --> 00:28:58,014
There's actually this new place, the Rabowitz,
which is kind of the place for abstract expressionism.

402
00:28:58,038 --> 00:29:01,034
Does that, uh... Does
that sound good to you?

403
00:29:03,770 --> 00:29:06,037
- Maybe you have
something else in mind?

404
00:29:06,061 --> 00:29:09,340
<i>♪ Round, round, get around,
I get around, yeah ♪</i>

405
00:29:09,364 --> 00:29:12,083
<i>♪ Get around, round, round,
I get around ♪</i>

406
00:29:12,107 --> 00:29:17,748
<i>♪ I get around,
from town to town ♪</i>

407
00:29:17,772 --> 00:29:20,899
<i>♪ I'm a real cool head ♪</i>

408
00:29:20,923 --> 00:29:24,892
<i>♪ I'm making real good bread ♪</i>

409
00:29:24,916 --> 00:29:28,244
<i>♪ I'm getting bugged driving
up and down the same old strip ♪</i>

410
00:29:28,268 --> 00:29:31,728
<i>♪ I gotta find a new place
where the kids are hip ♪</i>

411
00:29:35,358 --> 00:29:38,359
<i>♪ My buddies and me
are getting real well-known... ♪</i>

412
00:29:48,037 --> 00:29:49,695
[EXCLAIMING]

413
00:29:52,377 --> 00:29:54,928
[BORED SIGHING]

414
00:29:56,056 --> 00:29:59,138
[ALL LAUGHING]

415
00:30:05,235 --> 00:30:07,018
[LAUGHING]

416
00:30:07,042 --> 00:30:08,305
Oh!

417
00:30:08,329 --> 00:30:11,095
[SNORING]

418
00:30:13,352 --> 00:30:14,844
Incredible.

419
00:30:15,574 --> 00:30:17,388
- You set?
- [STARTLED GRUNT]

420
00:30:25,339 --> 00:30:28,841
Why don't you get another ticket?
Could be more fun second time round.

421
00:30:47,703 --> 00:30:49,151
Okay.

422
00:30:52,648 --> 00:30:55,394
Brace yourself.

423
00:31:02,655 --> 00:31:05,938
[ALL SCREAMING]

424
00:31:08,541 --> 00:31:10,208
[WOMAN YELLS]

425
00:31:11,506 --> 00:31:14,303
[SCREAMING]

426
00:31:16,360 --> 00:31:18,211
[MAN YELLS]

427
00:31:55,834 --> 00:31:57,579
[SIGHS]

428
00:32:11,600 --> 00:32:12,796
[EXCLAIMS]

429
00:32:13,428 --> 00:32:14,842
[EXCLAIMS]

430
00:32:22,748 --> 00:32:26,479
- He's a genius, huh?
- That's what they tell me.

431
00:32:34,863 --> 00:32:37,366
Well, he looks like
a fruitcake to me.

432
00:32:40,984 --> 00:32:43,567
Willing to take
responsibility for his actions?

433
00:32:45,206 --> 00:32:47,079
- [EXCLAIMS]
- [GASPS]

434
00:32:47,439 --> 00:32:49,780
Well, you're a braver
man than me,

435
00:32:49,804 --> 00:32:53,462
 and I single-handedly take
on gangs armed with AK-47s.

436
00:32:55,829 --> 00:33:00,268
Get him out of here. Tell him his is
a butt I would dearly love to kick,

437
00:33:00,930 --> 00:33:03,104
and the next time, I will.

438
00:33:07,132 --> 00:33:12,537
Doctor, there is something
we have to talk about,

439
00:33:13,149 --> 00:33:16,771
- and I don't quite
know how to put this...

440
00:33:16,795 --> 00:33:18,310
- [HUMMING]

441
00:33:18,310 --> 00:33:21,194
- What I am concerned about, is--
- [DOORBELL RINGS]

442
00:33:21,218 --> 00:33:23,678
Whoever that is, I am
getting rid of them.

443
00:33:26,854 --> 00:33:29,193
There he is!

444
00:33:29,217 --> 00:33:31,759
David. Sorry we're late.

445
00:33:32,459 --> 00:33:34,256
[AIR KISSING]

446
00:33:34,280 --> 00:33:37,317
- The traffic! Oh, terrible.
- Oh, that's beautiful!

447
00:33:37,341 --> 00:33:38,785
- Every day, it gets
worse and worse.

448
00:33:38,809 --> 00:33:42,029
- It's just the way she... It's the green.
- And why? Because nobody's in control.

449
00:33:42,053 --> 00:33:46,090
No, it's the yellow! It's just...
It's the yellow. She is a...

450
00:33:46,114 --> 00:33:48,613
- Well, where is Alison?
- Well, uh,

451
00:33:48,637 --> 00:33:51,349
I've got some bad news
and some good news.

452
00:33:51,559 --> 00:33:54,993
The bad news is that Alison
has had to go visit her mom,

453
00:33:55,017 --> 00:33:57,992
who, well, is not well.

454
00:33:58,016 --> 00:33:59,737
- That's too bad.
- Gee, the poor thing.

455
00:33:59,761 --> 00:34:02,130
- Sorry to hear that, David.
- The good news,

456
00:34:02,154 --> 00:34:04,931
is that Dr Bean and
I will be cooking.

457
00:34:04,955 --> 00:34:07,111
- Oh! [LAUGHING]
- You sure that's good news?

458
00:34:07,135 --> 00:34:09,473
Well, of course it is!

459
00:34:09,497 --> 00:34:12,154
- All the greatest chefs
in the world are men.

460
00:34:12,585 --> 00:34:15,477
So what's on the
menu, maestro?

461
00:34:15,865 --> 00:34:18,023
How could I forget?

462
00:34:19,016 --> 00:34:20,872
What the hell are we
going to give them?

463
00:34:22,741 --> 00:34:26,197
Yes, I think they may be expecting
something slightly more formal.

464
00:34:29,769 --> 00:34:32,005
- An onion? Just on its own?

465
00:34:34,181 --> 00:34:37,744
No, no, don't be ridiculous. That's for
Thanksgiving, and Alison would kill me.

466
00:34:37,768 --> 00:34:40,141
No, no. There must
be something else.

467
00:34:42,855 --> 00:34:46,728
- Have you ever cooked one of these before?
- Oh, yes.

468
00:34:46,752 --> 00:34:49,285
But it- it would take
like five hours, right?

469
00:34:50,001 --> 00:34:52,638
Not necessarily...

470
00:34:55,512 --> 00:35:00,330
[GRUNTING]

471
00:35:01,900 --> 00:35:03,932
What do you think?
Twenty minutes?

472
00:35:03,956 --> 00:35:05,304
I- I don't know.

473
00:35:07,494 --> 00:35:09,510
- You're sure this is gonna work?
- [MUTTERING]

474
00:35:09,534 --> 00:35:12,629
Okay, you're the chef. Just put on some
vegetables and come and say hello.

475
00:35:17,562 --> 00:35:20,543
Oh! Maestro.

476
00:35:20,567 --> 00:35:24,983
- My taste buds are positively
tingling with anticipation.

477
00:35:32,071 --> 00:35:33,695
No. No, thanks.

478
00:35:34,685 --> 00:35:37,003
- I think we'll pass
on the appetizer.

479
00:35:39,063 --> 00:35:41,513
So, Bean...

480
00:35:41,537 --> 00:35:45,251
Big day, Tuesday. Your
big day, my big day.

481
00:35:46,482 --> 00:35:49,834
I have a question for you.
[MICROWAVE BEEPING]

482
00:35:54,729 --> 00:35:57,662
- He's very, uh,
attentive to detail.

483
00:35:58,718 --> 00:36:01,134
- Everything must be just so.

484
00:36:01,635 --> 00:36:03,445
[LOUD EXPLOSION]

485
00:36:19,811 --> 00:36:23,222
- Bean, I think we need to
be honest with each other.

486
00:36:23,682 --> 00:36:27,158
I cannot deny that,
over the last days,

487
00:36:27,158 --> 00:36:31,492
some suspicions have begun
to gather in my mind,

488
00:36:31,516 --> 00:36:35,113
and I would just like to ask
you some direct questions.

489
00:36:35,544 --> 00:36:36,640
- Okay?

490
00:36:37,623 --> 00:36:39,853
Number one...

491
00:36:40,493 --> 00:36:42,233
Are you a doctor?

492
00:36:49,128 --> 00:36:50,502
No.

493
00:36:53,449 --> 00:36:55,175
Number two...

494
00:36:55,716 --> 00:36:58,799
- Do you know
anything about art?

495
00:37:01,271 --> 00:37:02,441
Um...

496
00:37:03,728 --> 00:37:05,111
Well...

497
00:37:06,220 --> 00:37:09,198
Just for instance here, um...

498
00:37:09,222 --> 00:37:10,780
[CHUCKLES]

499
00:37:11,249 --> 00:37:14,992
Was Leonardo da Vinci an
American basketball player?

500
00:37:15,016 --> 00:37:16,230
[LAUGHS]

501
00:37:26,963 --> 00:37:28,282
Yes.

502
00:37:41,086 --> 00:37:42,856
- Hi, Dad.
- Hi, Dad.

503
00:37:43,367 --> 00:37:44,561
Hello, Doctor.

504
00:37:44,585 --> 00:37:46,219
- Bye, Dad.
- Bye, Dad.

505
00:37:47,694 --> 00:37:49,301
[DOOR CLOSES]

506
00:37:50,951 --> 00:37:52,496
[SIGHING]

507
00:37:55,465 --> 00:37:57,642
<i>♪ Yesterday ♪</i>

508
00:37:58,700 --> 00:38:03,145
<i>♪ All my troubles
seemed so far away ♪</i>

509
00:38:03,732 --> 00:38:07,589
<i>♪ Now it looks as though
they're here to stay ♪</i>

510
00:38:07,613 --> 00:38:13,198
- <i>♪ Oh, I believe in yesterday ♪</i>
- Yep.

511
00:38:13,222 --> 00:38:15,478
<i>♪ Suddenly ♪</i>

512
00:38:16,473 --> 00:38:20,807
<i>♪ I'm not half the
man I used to be ♪</i>

513
00:38:21,743 --> 00:38:25,555
<i>♪ There's a shadow
hanging over me ♪</i>

514
00:38:25,579 --> 00:38:31,235
<i>♪ Oh, yesterday
came suddenly ♪</i>

515
00:38:31,259 --> 00:38:35,891
<i>♪ Why she
had to go ♪</i>

516
00:38:35,915 --> 00:38:39,467
<i>♪ I don't know,
she wouldn't say ♪</i>

517
00:38:39,491 --> 00:38:43,187
[SCREAMING]

518
00:38:43,211 --> 00:38:44,747
[GRUNTS]

519
00:38:44,798 --> 00:38:47,198
For God's sake, Bean,
what are you doing?

520
00:38:50,103 --> 00:38:53,911
[PHONE RINGING]

521
00:38:55,855 --> 00:38:57,303
Hello?

522
00:38:57,839 --> 00:39:01,309
Mr Grierson, yes. He's just
here in the shower with me.

523
00:39:01,333 --> 00:39:03,589
Would you give me--

524
00:39:04,412 --> 00:39:06,188
Yes, hello, Mr Grierson.

525
00:39:07,340 --> 00:39:11,200
Oh no, no, you must have
misunderstood him. What can I--

526
00:39:11,524 --> 00:39:14,892
She is? Oh, thank
you for calling me.

527
00:39:14,916 --> 00:39:18,034
- Yes, okay. Goodbye.
- Bye!

528
00:39:23,858 --> 00:39:28,070
I can't believe it. Whistler's Mother
has actually arrived in America.

529
00:39:28,272 --> 00:39:30,319
Now, listen,
I am begging you,

530
00:39:30,343 --> 00:39:33,619
as a man whose entire
professional career depends on you,

531
00:39:33,643 --> 00:39:35,674
behave rationally today.

532
00:39:38,824 --> 00:39:40,634
There is no God.

533
00:40:09,778 --> 00:40:11,344
What do you think?

534
00:40:14,942 --> 00:40:17,014
Nice frame.

535
00:40:17,038 --> 00:40:18,903
She's magnificent.

536
00:40:20,223 --> 00:40:24,498
David, well done. Worth
every bright green buck!

537
00:40:24,752 --> 00:40:26,689
She's something else, isn't she?

538
00:40:29,424 --> 00:40:32,878
All right, folks. Enjoy her while you can.
After this morning, we lock her away

539
00:40:32,902 --> 00:40:35,683
until the guy who paid
$50 million for her turns up.

540
00:40:35,683 --> 00:40:36,767
[LAUGHING]

541
00:40:36,791 --> 00:40:39,093
And, Elmer, I believe we
have a security meeting.

542
00:40:39,349 --> 00:40:40,610
That's right, sir.

543
00:40:40,634 --> 00:40:44,578
And, ah!-- This is your key
to the encasement system.

544
00:40:44,602 --> 00:40:46,625
There are only two.
I have the other one.

545
00:40:46,649 --> 00:40:50,218
So, if the painting turns up
missing, I'll know where to come.

546
00:40:50,242 --> 00:40:51,638
[LAUGHING]

547
00:40:51,662 --> 00:40:54,246
And if you could all join
me at the security console?

548
00:40:54,270 --> 00:40:55,852
Doctor, why don't
you just stay here?

549
00:40:55,876 --> 00:40:59,551
Maybe a look at the real thing
will inspire you for your speech.

550
00:41:02,213 --> 00:41:05,384
Just stay here. Don't leave
this room till I get back, okay?

551
00:41:13,825 --> 00:41:15,418
[SIGHING]

552
00:41:21,840 --> 00:41:25,604
This chart maps all the
security elements for the big day.

553
00:41:26,231 --> 00:41:29,167
And as usual in these circumstances,
I've given the operation a code name,

554
00:41:29,191 --> 00:41:31,666
which is Operation
Whistler's Mother.

555
00:41:32,322 --> 00:41:35,267
It's not a snazzy title,
but I think it works.

556
00:41:47,020 --> 00:41:48,844
[TAPPING]

557
00:41:53,607 --> 00:41:55,011
Tss!

558
00:41:55,035 --> 00:41:57,394
[BLOWING]

559
00:41:58,576 --> 00:42:01,202
Aah... aah... aah... aah...

560
00:42:08,054 --> 00:42:09,864
[SIGHING]

561
00:42:10,287 --> 00:42:12,359
