brings down the whole gallery.

570
00:42:58,114 --> 00:43:02,304
[GRUNTING]

571
00:43:11,699 --> 00:43:14,024
[HOCKING]

572
00:43:14,503 --> 00:43:17,625
[HOCKING]

573
00:43:19,769 --> 00:43:22,523
[EXCLAIMING]
No, no, no, no, no, no, no!

574
00:43:23,552 --> 00:43:26,000
[GRUNTING]

575
00:43:41,501 --> 00:43:45,386
[FAKE SQUEAKING SOUNDS]

576
00:43:49,771 --> 00:43:54,830
[FAKE SQUEAKING SOUNDS]

577
00:44:00,762 --> 00:44:03,252
[REAL SQUEAKING SOUNDS]

578
00:44:03,723 --> 00:44:06,135
[FAKE SQUEAKING SOUNDS]

579
00:44:06,756 --> 00:44:08,838
Ooh!

580
00:44:12,258 --> 00:44:16,440
[FAKE SQUEAKING SOUNDS]

581
00:44:21,666 --> 00:44:24,285
Once we've gotten
the general safely inside,

582
00:44:24,309 --> 00:44:26,722
the key figure,
security-wise, will be Dr Bean.

583
00:44:26,746 --> 00:44:30,054
- We gotta protect the painting without
getting in the good doctor's way.

584
00:44:49,796 --> 00:44:51,003
Ah.

585
00:45:14,542 --> 00:45:16,056
[SIGHS IN RELIEF]

586
00:45:35,379 --> 00:45:37,626
[CANS TUMBLING]
[EXCLAIMS]

587
00:45:40,755 --> 00:45:44,092
[PANICKED STUTTERING]

588
00:45:53,202 --> 00:45:55,546
[WHIMPERING]

589
00:46:03,378 --> 00:46:08,506
[WHIMPERING]

590
00:46:10,242 --> 00:46:13,773
[EXCLAIMING]

591
00:46:28,740 --> 00:46:31,051
[KNOCKING]

592
00:46:37,516 --> 00:46:39,837
There seems to be
a problem with the...

593
00:46:40,540 --> 00:46:42,552
[PANICKED STUTTERING]

594
00:46:43,988 --> 00:46:45,332
door...

595
00:47:01,072 --> 00:47:03,013
Where's the picture gone?

596
00:47:04,937 --> 00:47:06,065
Um...

597
00:47:06,811 --> 00:47:08,490
[STUTTERING]

598
00:47:08,832 --> 00:47:10,326
What? what, what, what?

599
00:47:21,629 --> 00:47:24,791
Oh, Jesus! Oh, God!
Oh- Oh, Jesus!

600
00:47:24,815 --> 00:47:29,040
- God! Oh, Mary, mother of
Jesus! Jesus of Nazareth!

601
00:47:34,494 --> 00:47:37,470
Wait a minute. Why am I
worrying about this? You did it!

602
00:47:37,494 --> 00:47:39,729
- All I have to do is go out and
tell them what happened!

603
00:47:40,798 --> 00:47:42,375
No, perfect!

604
00:47:42,399 --> 00:47:44,790
Then they'll say, "Well, who left
him alone with the picture?"

605
00:47:44,814 --> 00:47:48,711
And I say, "Me." Then they say,
"Well, you're fired!" I say, "Fine."

606
00:47:48,735 --> 00:47:51,364
No, no, no, no, no! Then they say,
"Firing is not enough."

607
00:47:51,388 --> 00:47:53,182
- "Let's prosecute you
for negligence!"

608
00:47:53,206 --> 00:47:57,058
Then I go to jail. My wife leaves me.
My daughter becomes a prostitute.

609
00:47:57,082 --> 00:48:01,142
I wind up on death row, sharing
a cell with Butch McDick!

610
00:48:01,166 --> 00:48:03,555
- All right, calm down.
I just gotta calm down.

611
00:48:03,579 --> 00:48:05,148
Okay. Okay.

612
00:48:05,172 --> 00:48:06,906
Give me one more
look at the painting.

613
00:48:08,813 --> 00:48:12,057
- Oh, God! Oh, God!
Oh, God! Oh, God!

614
00:48:12,081 --> 00:48:15,519
...which renders the underground
ventilation ducts completely secure.

615
00:48:15,543 --> 00:48:18,354
- Yes, well, that's good to know.
- Thank you, sir.

616
00:48:41,439 --> 00:48:43,561
Hey, do you drink, Bean?

617
00:48:43,986 --> 00:48:46,872
- No.
- Good. Neither do I.

618
00:48:53,989 --> 00:48:56,885
[TV] <i>Yeah, this one has extra
innings written all over it, doesn't it?</i>

619
00:48:57,316 --> 00:48:58,864
<i>All right, he hands
over the plate.</i>

620
00:48:58,888 --> 00:49:01,360
<i>He's got the long-ball
threat from the left side.</i>

621
00:49:02,235 --> 00:49:04,627
<i>Smith corks one to
right field down the line.</i>

622
00:49:04,651 --> 00:49:06,198
- <i>There it goes.</i>
- Bean?

623
00:49:06,222 --> 00:49:07,592
<i>It's going!
[CHEERING]</i>

624
00:49:08,078 --> 00:49:12,549
<i>Going crazy, folks!
Going crazy! It's a home run!</i>

625
00:49:12,573 --> 00:49:14,843
<i>And the Cardinals
have won the...</i>

626
00:49:14,867 --> 00:49:17,008
[BLEATING]

627
00:49:19,016 --> 00:49:20,429
Carry on.

628
00:49:24,985 --> 00:49:28,355
You wanna know what the
reality of the situation is?

629
00:49:28,379 --> 00:49:31,113
Okay, this is it.

630
00:49:32,234 --> 00:49:35,714
Because you moved into my
house, my wife has moved out.

631
00:49:36,237 --> 00:49:41,852
Yes, she did. She might have moved
out anyway, but that is one- point one.

632
00:49:41,876 --> 00:49:43,625
Point two,

633
00:49:45,083 --> 00:49:50,010
I've given my life to art,
and from here on in,

634
00:49:50,596 --> 00:49:55,787
the only art I will get anywhere near
are the pictures I draw on the pavement,

635
00:49:55,811 --> 00:49:59,595
hoping passers-by will
throw nickels in my hat.

636
00:49:59,964 --> 00:50:02,316
I guess the long
and the short of it,

637
00:50:03,844 --> 00:50:05,935
I wish I'd never been born.

638
00:50:19,288 --> 00:50:23,430
- Yesterday...

639
00:50:23,430 --> 00:50:30,064
all my troubles
seemed so far away...

640
00:50:30,906 --> 00:50:36,321
Now it looks as though
they're here to stay.

641
00:50:36,345 --> 00:50:39,584
I believe...

642
00:50:40,245 --> 00:50:42,710
 in yesterday.

643
00:50:42,991 --> 00:50:44,976
Suddenly!

644
00:50:45,000 --> 00:50:50,159
- I'm not half the man I used to be!
- Oh, no.

645
00:50:50,280 --> 00:50:55,566
There's a shadow
hanging over me.

646
00:50:55,590 --> 00:51:01,833
I believe in yesterday.

647
00:51:02,141 --> 00:51:05,699
- Suddenly!
- Mmm...

648
00:51:08,207 --> 00:51:13,375
- [BOTH] Row, row, row, your boat,
gently down the stream,

649
00:51:13,399 --> 00:51:18,186
[BOTH] If you see a crocodile,
don't forget to scream!

650
00:51:18,186 --> 00:51:21,686
- Ah!
- Splish, splash, splish, splash.

651
00:51:21,710 --> 00:51:28,028
[BOTH] Row, row, row your boat,
gently down the stream,

652
00:51:28,052 --> 00:51:33,042
If you see a crocodile,
don't forget to scream!

653
00:51:33,066 --> 00:51:34,381
[SCREAMING]

654
00:51:34,403 --> 00:51:35,653
[SIGHS]

655
00:51:35,884 --> 00:51:40,054
- I can explain everything.
- Okay, explain everything.

656
00:51:42,880 --> 00:51:45,918
- It's inexplicable. Ali, listen--
- David, no.

657
00:51:45,942 --> 00:51:49,418
David, the reason that I
came home was because

658
00:51:49,442 --> 00:51:54,623
I missed you. And also because
I live here. And they live here.

659
00:51:54,647 --> 00:52:00,143
- but, David, we have been waiting
for five hours, and what do we get?

660
00:52:00,167 --> 00:52:03,329
A drunk, and his friend
from the planet Zog!

661
00:52:03,353 --> 00:52:08,727
- I know, and it seems like this
horrible situation, but if you could...

662
00:52:08,727 --> 00:52:13,425
If you could just hear
my full horrific tale...

663
00:52:13,425 --> 00:52:18,360
David, I think I'm
gonna have to leave you.

664
00:52:19,592 --> 00:52:21,177
You are?

665
00:52:24,765 --> 00:52:28,382
Well, then things really
have gotten bad.

666
00:52:31,536 --> 00:52:32,809
Yeah.

667
00:52:56,070 --> 00:52:57,658
- Hi, Beanie.

668
00:53:01,010 --> 00:53:02,535
Can't sleep, huh?

669
00:53:03,392 --> 00:53:04,520
No.

670
00:53:07,120 --> 00:53:08,825
Me, neither.

671
00:53:08,849 --> 00:53:11,052
Can't stop thinking
about naked women.

672
00:53:12,531 --> 00:53:16,534
- What about you?
- Whistler's Mother.

673
00:53:16,558 --> 00:53:18,484
Well, whatever turns you on.

674
00:53:25,210 --> 00:53:27,076
Catch you around, moon-man.

675
00:53:29,211 --> 00:53:32,106
Oh, if you still can't
sleep, come to my room.

676
00:53:32,130 --> 00:53:35,324
 I got some great posters of
Cindy Crawford on my wall.

677
00:54:33,024 --> 00:54:35,397
[EXCLAIMING]

678
00:55:47,140 --> 00:55:50,231
[GROANING]

679
00:56:06,466 --> 00:56:08,780
[GROANING]

680
00:56:10,276 --> 00:56:11,651
Come on!

681
00:56:17,117 --> 00:56:19,431
[GROANING]

682
00:57:38,143 --> 00:57:41,079
[GROANING]

683
00:58:03,521 --> 00:58:05,796
[LOUD BANGING]

684
00:58:07,547 --> 00:58:12,096
[GROANING]

685
00:58:22,183 --> 00:58:24,465
- [GUARD SCREAMING]

686
00:58:24,489 --> 00:58:26,265
[THUD]
[GRUNTS]

687
00:58:27,460 --> 00:58:29,643
[GROANING]

688
00:58:29,895 --> 00:58:31,303
[SIGHING IN RELIEF]

689
00:58:31,488 --> 00:58:33,848
Hooray!
[PANG]

690
00:58:33,848 --> 00:58:35,435
Ah!

691
00:58:38,916 --> 00:58:40,166
Ah!

692
00:58:43,480 --> 00:58:46,981
Oh, my God!
Oh, my God!

693
00:58:47,382 --> 00:58:49,860
[TIRES SCREECHING]

694
00:58:49,884 --> 00:58:53,188
[ENGINE RUMBLING]

695
00:58:54,813 --> 00:58:56,197
Jennifer!

696
00:58:56,944 --> 00:58:59,953
No, no, no, no, no! Don't think for one
minute you're getting on that bike, Jen.

697
00:58:59,977 --> 00:59:01,462
Morning, everyone.

698
00:59:01,951 --> 00:59:03,408
[STRETCHES]

699
00:59:03,432 --> 00:59:06,499
- Total family rule, here. No one
is allowed on one of those death traps.

700
00:59:06,523 --> 00:59:10,531
- You promised me, Jen.
- You promised mom you'd get rid of Dr Freako!

701
00:59:10,555 --> 00:59:14,506
[ENGINE ROARING]

702
00:59:16,712 --> 00:59:18,016
[CHUCKLES]

703
00:59:26,313 --> 00:59:27,957
- Um--
- Don't talk to me.

704
00:59:27,981 --> 00:59:30,242
I am trying to think
what I'm going to do.

705
00:59:35,165 --> 00:59:36,679
- Well...
- Quiet!

706
00:59:52,639 --> 00:59:53,866
- There they are!

707
01:00:11,018 --> 01:00:14,489
- What a pleasure, sir.
Welcome to our humble abode.

708
01:00:14,513 --> 01:00:16,916
Not too humble, I hope.
I'm expecting lunch.

709
01:00:16,940 --> 01:00:20,562
- Of course. General, right this way.
- After you, sir. After you.

710
01:00:21,298 --> 01:00:24,426
[REPORTERS CHATTERING]

711
01:00:26,568 --> 01:00:27,870
Glad you could make it.

712
01:00:29,745 --> 01:00:32,045
- Where the hell have you been?
- I am sorry. Trouble at home.

713
01:00:32,069 --> 01:00:34,081
- Never mind. Come along.
- Sir, there is something...

714
01:00:34,105 --> 01:00:36,728
General, I'd like you to meet
our curator, David Langley.

715
01:00:36,752 --> 01:00:38,885
- I'm delighted to meet you, sir.
- Of course you are.

716
01:00:39,143 --> 01:00:40,687
Right through here, sir.

717
01:00:53,447 --> 01:00:57,325
Ladies and gentlemen, this is
a great moment for America.

718
01:00:57,349 --> 01:01:01,196
And for one very special
lady, Whistler's Mother.

719
01:01:01,220 --> 01:01:04,884
[CLAPPING]

720
01:01:05,480 --> 01:01:09,462
- And she's here today, thanks
to our great and good benefactor.

721
01:01:09,923 --> 01:01:16,298
Will you join me in saluting a
soldier, philanthropist, loving father...

722
01:01:17,116 --> 01:01:20,812
and an extremely generous patron
of the arts, General Newton?

723
01:01:20,836 --> 01:01:23,188
[CLAPPING]

724
01:01:23,627 --> 01:01:25,073
Thank you, ladies and gentlemen.

725
01:01:26,367 --> 01:01:29,580
I am not a traditional art lover.

726
01:01:29,604 --> 01:01:33,140
I don't know the difference
between a Picasso and a car crash.

727
01:01:33,596 --> 01:01:35,256
- But I love my country.

728
01:01:35,280 --> 01:01:40,248
I can't stand the idea of a bunch of Frenchies
owning America's greatest painting.

729
01:01:40,462 --> 01:01:41,590
 Let's get on with it.

730
01:01:50,571 --> 01:01:52,218
Welcome home, Ma.

731
01:02:08,731 --> 01:02:14,854
[CHEERING, CLAPPING]

732
01:02:22,970 --> 01:02:26,133
Whatever you did, you're
a genius, and I love you!

733
01:02:26,570 --> 01:02:28,068
It's a poster.

734
01:02:28,801 --> 01:02:31,559
It's a what?
- David, it's time.

735
01:02:31,583 --> 01:02:33,646
- Uh, t- time for what?
- The speech.

736
01:02:33,670 --> 01:02:35,946
- Wha-- I, Wait, what speech?
- Congratulations, David!

737
01:02:35,970 --> 01:02:37,789
Dr Bean's speech
about the painting.

738
01:02:37,813 --> 01:02:39,609
Yes, of course, the speech.

739
01:02:42,114 --> 01:02:44,926
- Now, Doctor, try to
keep it below an hour,

740
01:02:44,926 --> 01:02:47,515
and if there could be one
joke in it, that would be nice.

741
01:02:47,980 --> 01:02:52,189
Good Morning America couldn't give
a flying monkey about "isms" and, uh...

742
01:02:52,523 --> 01:02:54,538
- Oh, you know what I mean.
- Yeah.

743
01:02:55,614 --> 01:02:57,295
[PANICKED STUTTERING]

744
01:02:57,615 --> 01:02:59,973
- lt doesn't have to go on for long. Just...
- Dr Bean?

745
01:02:59,997 --> 01:03:04,261
- We're ready for you. Right this way.
- Just... Just... Just... Just... Just... Just... Just...

746
01:03:04,811 --> 01:03:08,283
- And now, for the second
highlight of our day.

747
01:03:08,307 --> 01:03:12,894
Dr Bean, a great English art scholar,
is here to speak for a few minutes,

748
01:03:12,918 --> 01:03:16,555
not too long, Doctor, on the
subject of our new purchase.

749
01:03:16,579 --> 01:03:19,773
Ladies and gentlemen, Dr Bean
of the National Gallery of England.

750
01:03:19,797 --> 01:03:21,797
[CLAPPING]

751
01:03:29,376 --> 01:03:30,376
...

752
01:03:30,807 --> 01:03:33,018
[CHUCKLES NERVOUSLY]

753
01:03:33,042 --> 01:03:36,625
Um... Uh...

754
01:03:36,649 --> 01:03:38,302
- Well...

755
01:03:38,326 --> 01:03:43,951
Hello. I'm Dr Bean,
apparently...

756
01:03:45,953 --> 01:03:48,900
And- and my job,

757
01:03:48,924 --> 01:03:52,554
is to sit and
look at paintings.

758
01:03:52,578 --> 01:03:55,760
[CLAPPING]
- [MAN] Just him and the painting?

759
01:03:56,388 --> 01:03:59,000
[CLAPPING]

760
01:03:59,882 --> 01:04:03,237
Um, So... Um,

761
01:04:03,261 --> 01:04:10,183
what have I learned that
I can say about this painting?

762
01:04:11,476 --> 01:04:12,724
Um...

763
01:04:14,697 --> 01:04:19,785
Well... well, firstly,
it's quite big.

764
01:04:19,809 --> 01:04:22,270
- which is excellent.
- Mm-hmm.

765
01:04:22,294 --> 01:04:25,376
- Because if it was really small,

766
01:04:25,400 --> 01:04:28,612
you know, microscopic,

767
01:04:28,636 --> 01:04:34,672
then hardly anybody would be able to
see it, which would be a tremendous shame.

768
01:04:35,697 --> 01:04:37,897
Um, secondly,

769
01:04:39,016 --> 01:04:43,504
and I'm getting quite
near the end now of this...

770
01:04:43,528 --> 01:04:47,138
analysis of this painting...

771
01:04:48,219 --> 01:04:50,785
Secondly,

772
01:04:54,271 --> 01:04:58,398
why was it worth
this man, here,

773
01:04:58,422 --> 01:05:05,281
- spending 50 million of your
American dollars on this portrait?

774
01:05:05,933 --> 01:05:09,851
- And, and, and the
answer is, um...

775
01:05:09,875 --> 01:05:17,081
- Well, this picture is worth
such a lot of money because...

776
01:05:20,525 --> 01:05:23,381
it's a picture...

777
01:05:23,405 --> 01:05:27,139
of Whistler's mother.

778
01:05:27,933 --> 01:05:35,043
- And as I've learned by
staying with my best friend,

779
01:05:35,067 --> 01:05:38,527
- David Langley, and his family,

780
01:05:38,551 --> 01:05:42,783
families are very important.

781
01:05:43,196 --> 01:05:48,763
- and even though Mr Whistler
was perfectly aware...

782
01:05:49,146 --> 01:05:57,101
that his mother was a hideous old bat who
looked like she had a cactus lodged up her backside,

783
01:05:57,125 --> 01:06:00,385
- [CHUCKLING]
- he stuck with her.

784
01:06:00,409 --> 01:06:06,306
And even took the time to
paint this amazing picture of her.

785
01:06:07,378 --> 01:06:09,513
It's not just a painting.

786
01:06:10,566 --> 01:06:15,039
It's a picture of
a mad old cow,

787
01:06:15,420 --> 01:06:20,933
- who he thought the world
of, and that's marvelous.

788
01:06:20,957 --> 01:06:23,083
- [MAN] Well said.
- [CROWD CHATTERING]

789
01:06:23,107 --> 01:06:25,193
Well, that's what
I think, anyway.

790
01:06:25,217 --> 01:06:32,188
[CLAPPING]

791
01:06:59,913 --> 01:07:03,013
- Home and dry.
- Where did you put the original?

792
01:07:03,922 --> 01:07:05,018
Uh...

793
01:07:05,925 --> 01:07:08,443
- Mr Langley, may
I have a word?

794
01:07:10,179 --> 01:07:11,656
- Stay here. Shut up.

795
01:07:12,062 --> 01:07:14,772
- Mr Langley--
- Okay, I can explain everything.

796
01:07:15,333 --> 01:07:17,229
- What can you explain?
- About the painting.

797
01:07:17,253 --> 01:07:19,140
- Which painting?
- Whistler's Mother.

798
01:07:20,109 --> 01:07:24,596
I'm afraid, Mr Langley, I'm here in my capacity
as a police officer, not as an art lover.

799
01:07:24,620 --> 01:07:28,599
It's about your daughter. She's
been in a motorcycle accident.

800
01:07:30,762 --> 01:07:33,226
[SIRENS WAILING]

801
01:07:34,946 --> 01:07:38,215
[SIRENS WAILING]

802
01:07:48,871 --> 01:07:50,883
[TIRES SCREECHING]

803
01:07:53,863 --> 01:07:56,527
[SIRENS WAILING]

804
01:07:56,551 --> 01:07:59,872
- Damn! Request emergency
clearance for 809-Adam.

805
01:07:59,896 --> 01:08:01,012
Shut up!

806
01:08:02,946 --> 01:08:07,403
- 211 in progress. Suspect male.
Possibly armed. Request immediate backup.

807
01:08:07,427 --> 01:08:09,748
Keep speeding! If you get
a ticket, I'll tear it up.

808
01:08:09,772 --> 01:08:11,054
- Thanks.
- Hold it!

809
01:08:11,078 --> 01:08:13,888
- Hey, easy, fella, easy.
Hold it, there. Right there.

810
01:08:13,912 --> 01:08:15,857
Whoa! Easy!

811
01:08:15,881 --> 01:08:17,953
Put it down. Put the gun down.

812
01:08:18,548 --> 01:08:19,868
[GUNSHOT]

813
01:08:22,503 --> 01:08:27,563
Hi, I need Jennifer Langley's room. She came
in at about 11:00, motorcycle accident.

814
01:08:29,172 --> 01:08:30,840
- Out of the way, sir.
- [GRUNTS]

815
01:08:38,443 --> 01:08:41,248
[GROANING]

816
01:08:42,739 --> 01:08:46,497
She's on the fifth floor, east
wing. Just take the elevator up.

817
01:08:46,821 --> 01:08:47,883
Oh!

818
01:08:47,907 --> 01:08:49,215
Just...

819
01:08:49,976 --> 01:08:53,346
All right.
[EXCLAIMING]

820
01:08:54,697 --> 01:08:56,686
I am so--
Just--

821
01:08:57,060 --> 01:09:01,116
Please, just stay
here and do nothing!

822
01:09:01,140 --> 01:09:04,202
If you do nothing,
nothing can go wrong.

823
01:09:05,975 --> 01:09:08,180
I've got to take care
of my family now.

824
01:09:29,075 --> 01:09:33,812
The nurse says that it's
not a coma, that she's just...

825
01:09:33,836 --> 01:09:36,523
She's just sort of
taking time out.

826
01:09:37,571 --> 01:09:40,461
But we haven't seen a
doctor yet, so I don't...

827
01:09:46,887 --> 01:09:48,520
- Dr Jacobson?
- Yes?

828
01:09:48,544 --> 01:09:50,091
We need you urgently in OR 3.

829
01:09:50,115 --> 01:09:52,551
- No can do. I'm due in number 4 at 1:00.
- Sir, it's urgent!

830
01:09:52,575 --> 01:09:55,152
We have a ruptured
kidney and massive bleeding.

831
01:09:55,176 --> 01:09:56,405
Okay.

832
01:10:09,683 --> 01:10:11,246
Yes, it's urgent!

833
01:10:11,970 --> 01:10:14,548
- What's happening?
- Surgeon's on his way up.

834
01:10:16,248 --> 01:10:19,050
- At last, Dr... Bean.

835
01:10:19,074 --> 01:10:21,709
- Hello, Doctor. We've
got a 45-year-old male,

836
01:10:21,733 --> 01:10:24,530
- blood type B negative, brought in
by paramedics 10 minutes ago.

837
01:10:24,554 --> 01:10:26,846
- He's got a penetrating
injury to the left chest.

838
01:10:30,011 --> 01:10:32,295
- Gotta get him in there right now.
- No, no, no!

839
01:10:33,367 --> 01:10:36,250
Come in, Doctor. It looks like
a bullet in the lower thorax.

840
01:10:36,274 --> 01:10:39,172
It seems to have ruptured the lung, and
severe inner bleeding along the abdomen.

841
01:10:39,196 --> 01:10:41,666
- Doctor, he's coming to.
- All right, give him a T-70.

842
01:10:41,690 --> 01:10:42,750
- Got it.

843
01:10:46,444 --> 02:21:32,110
...

844
01:10:50,604 --> 01:10:53,123
- [CHUCKLES]
- [GRUNTS]

845
01:10:53,147 --> 01:10:54,727
Shall I extend the incision, sir?

846
01:10:54,727 --> 01:10:57,036
- We've taken it from the
breastbone to the diaphragm.

847
01:10:57,060 --> 01:10:59,203
We may have to extend
further to get to the spleen.

848
01:10:59,607 --> 01:11:01,486
[STUTTERING]

849
01:11:01,510 --> 01:11:03,004
Sorry, sir. It's only
my first week.

850
01:11:03,028 --> 01:11:04,375
[MUTTERING]

851
01:11:04,399 --> 01:11:06,543
No, you're right, Doctor.
We should wait for the x-rays.

852
01:11:07,518 --> 01:11:09,714
- Help! We are losing
this guy out here!

853
01:11:09,738 --> 01:11:13,387
- For Jesus' sake, we need help!
- Go! Go! Go! Get in here!

854
01:11:15,320 --> 01:11:17,271
Watch he doesn't go into
haemodynamic shock.

855
01:11:17,295 --> 01:11:18,500
We'll be right back.

856
01:11:26,046 --> 01:11:29,497
- Defibrillating!
- All right, charge the paddles to 200 watts.

857
01:11:32,997 --> 01:11:34,207
Clear!

858
01:11:40,380 --> 01:11:42,202
[SIGHING]

859
01:11:51,086 --> 01:11:52,199
[EXCLAIMS]

860
01:12:03,521 --> 01:12:05,085
[GROANS]

861
01:12:11,840 --> 01:12:13,193
Ah!

862
01:12:16,332 --> 01:12:17,805
What's that?

863
01:12:42,060 --> 01:12:43,913
Ah!
[CHUCKLES]

864
01:12:51,787 --> 01:12:53,962
Jolly good. Clean it up.

865
01:13:14,223 --> 01:13:15,651
Sorry to keep you waiting, sir.

866
01:13:15,675 --> 01:13:17,647
Nice work in there, people.
Now back to the bleeding!

867
01:13:17,671 --> 01:13:20,503
- Where's this blood coming from? We got to
clamp it off. Where the hell's that J-17?

868
01:13:20,527 --> 01:13:23,233
- We need that J-17 in OR 4 right now!
- He's got major trauma.

869
01:13:23,257 --> 01:13:25,107
There's too much blood.
I can't see a thing.

870
01:13:25,131 --> 01:13:27,745
- More suction!
- Somebody check that line.

871
01:13:27,769 --> 01:13:30,294
- Blood pressure's bottoming out.
- Hook me up with 12, will you? Now!

872
01:13:30,318 --> 01:13:32,399
- His heart rate is dropping.
We're gonna lose him.

873
01:13:33,286 --> 01:13:36,026
- How's the TMS?
- Come on, people! He's crashing on us.

874
01:13:36,050 --> 01:13:38,606
- Damn! I'm not getting a reading.
- Where is this bullet?

875
01:13:39,143 --> 01:13:40,159
- Come on, hold on to him!

876
01:13:40,183 --> 01:13:43,230
- If we don't find it, he's a dead man.
- I am not gonna lose this guy!

877
01:13:45,064 --> 01:13:47,625
Sir, sir, we're losing him!
I've got to get in there now.

878
01:13:52,662 --> 01:13:54,908
Doctor, you can't just...
It's too dangerous!

879
01:13:54,932 --> 01:13:56,735
Ah, ah, ah, ah!

880
01:14:09,209 --> 01:14:10,600
My God!

881
01:14:11,841 --> 01:14:13,890
[PANG]

882
01:14:14,087 --> 01:14:16,969
- Incredible.
- Heart rate is stabilizing.

883
01:14:17,230 --> 01:14:19,087
- Blood pressure's climbing back.

884
01:14:21,062 --> 01:14:24,091
[CLAPPING]

885
01:14:24,398 --> 01:14:25,872
[GAWKING]

886
01:14:33,538 --> 01:14:36,080
Oh, Doctor. Thank God. My
daughter was in an accident.

887
01:14:36,104 --> 01:14:39,740
No one's even taken a look at her yet. We're
desperate for some medical attention in here.

888
01:14:41,603 --> 01:14:43,002
Would you mind?

889
01:14:45,671 --> 01:14:47,332
[COUGHS]

890
01:14:47,356 --> 01:14:49,130
Don't worry, honey. The
doctor will check her out.

891
01:14:49,154 --> 01:14:50,410
Oh, thank God.

892
01:14:54,653 --> 01:14:56,386
- Mr and Mrs Langley,

893
01:14:56,410 --> 01:15:00,182
- any kind of damage to the brain
is a very sensitive thing to observe,

894
01:15:00,206 --> 01:15:03,727
so maybe we should just step outside and
give the doctor a little room to work.

895
01:15:03,751 --> 01:15:07,616
- The first nurse said we shouldn't think
of it as a coma but more like a deep sleep.

896
01:15:07,640 --> 01:15:10,456
- Yes, that's sort of true.
- Could I ask you a question?

897
01:15:21,435 --> 01:15:23,011
Jennifer?

898
01:15:23,583 --> 01:15:27,649
Wakey, wakey, breakfast's ready.

899
01:15:27,673 --> 01:15:29,757
Jennifer?

900
01:15:30,128 --> 01:15:31,822
- What time is it?

901
01:15:33,521 --> 01:15:35,083
It's almost 2:00.

902
01:15:35,399 --> 01:15:38,876
Can you turn the clock
back about 12 hours for me?

903
01:15:40,001 --> 01:15:41,434
Sure.

904
01:15:43,662 --> 01:15:47,579
[SCREAMS]

905
01:15:52,546 --> 01:15:56,763
Wake up! Wake up! Wake up!

906
01:15:57,948 --> 01:16:00,307
I don't know what
to say about Bean.

907
01:16:01,020 --> 01:16:04,819
He's clearly a force-10
disaster area, but...

908
01:16:05,970 --> 01:16:10,310
God help me, I like him.

909
01:16:13,499 --> 01:16:15,533
Now, having said that,

910
01:16:15,557 --> 01:16:21,520
there is no reason in the world why any of you
should ever have anything to do with him again.

911
01:16:21,544 --> 01:16:23,766
[GRUNTING]

912
01:16:32,202 --> 01:16:34,793
[FRUSTRATED SIGHING]

913
01:16:51,219 --> 01:16:54,921
[MACHINE POWERING UP]

914
01:16:58,224 --> 01:17:00,279
[SCREAMING]

915
01:17:01,853 --> 01:17:03,400
[EXCLAIMING]

916
01:17:03,691 --> 01:17:06,468
Mom? Dad? Anyone?

917
01:17:06,492 --> 01:17:08,224
Oh, my God, Jennifer!

918
01:17:08,248 --> 01:17:11,298
- Oh, honey. Sweetie.
- Honey! Oh, look at you.

919
01:17:11,322 --> 01:17:13,148
- What happened?
- You had an accident, honey,

920
01:17:13,172 --> 01:17:15,648
but everything is
gonna be okay.

921
01:17:15,672 --> 01:17:19,129
- But what happened to Stingo?
- Just bruises, collar bone.

922
01:17:19,153 --> 01:17:21,729
- He'll be fine, until I
get my hands on him.

923
01:17:21,753 --> 01:17:25,845
- Hey, Doctor, where are you going?
- Where do you think you're going?

924
01:17:25,845 --> 01:17:29,572
 How can we ever say thank you?
No, you've made us so happy.

925
01:17:29,596 --> 01:17:32,794
- Tell us, what can we do? We'll do anything.
- Anything.

926
01:17:40,972 --> 01:17:44,481
Well, I suppose you could
let me stay another week....

927
01:17:56,715 --> 01:17:59,579
Sunset Boulevard.

928
01:17:59,603 --> 01:18:03,240
<i>♪ Rollin' down to
the Imperial Highway ♪</i>

929
01:18:03,743 --> 01:18:06,586
<i>♪ With a big nasty
redhead at my side ♪</i>

930
01:18:07,098 --> 01:18:09,781
<i>♪ Santa Ana winds
blowing hot from the north ♪</i>

931
01:18:09,978 --> 01:18:11,963
<i>♪ And we was
born to ride ♪</i>

932
01:18:14,443 --> 01:18:17,622
<i>♪ Roll down the window,
put down the top ♪</i>

933
01:18:18,135 --> 01:18:19,957
<i>♪ Crank up the
Beach Boys, baby ♪</i>

934
01:18:19,981 --> 01:18:21,704
<i>♪ Don't let
the music stop ♪</i>

935
01:18:21,728 --> 01:18:23,666
<i>♪ We're gonna
ride it 'til we ♪</i>

936
01:18:23,690 --> 01:18:25,642
<i>♪ Just can't ride
it no more ♪</i>

937
01:18:25,955 --> 01:18:28,172
[TIRES SCREECHING]

938
01:18:29,286 --> 01:18:32,761
<i>♪ From the South Bay
to the Valley ♪</i>

939
01:18:32,968 --> 01:18:36,407
<i>♪ From the West Side
to the East Side ♪</i>

940
01:18:36,848 --> 01:18:39,850
<i>♪ Everybody's
very happy ♪</i>

941
01:18:40,174 --> 01:18:42,847
<i>♪ 'Cause the sun is
shining all the time ♪</i>

942
01:18:43,090 --> 01:18:45,173
<i>♪ Looks like another
perfect day ♪</i>

943
01:18:46,670 --> 01:18:48,390
<i>♪ I love L.A. ♪</i>

944
01:18:50,409 --> 01:18:51,819
<i>♪ We love it ♪</i>

945
01:18:54,150 --> 01:18:55,682
<i>♪ I love L.A. ♪</i>

946
01:18:57,765 --> 01:18:59,070
<i>♪ We love it ♪</i>

947
01:19:00,977 --> 01:19:02,723
- [HORN HONKING]
- <i>♪ We love it! ♪</i>

948
01:19:02,747 --> 01:19:05,959
- Presents! Presents!
- Hey! What's this all about?

949
01:19:07,707 --> 01:19:10,365
- This is heavy!
- Thank you.

950
01:19:14,443 --> 01:19:18,602
- Oh, look, laxatives.
Yeah, a whole box of them.

951
01:19:18,953 --> 01:19:20,915
Oh, hey! there they are!

952
01:19:21,410 --> 01:19:23,363
- Eggs. Six of them!

953
01:19:24,196 --> 01:19:26,031
Barbie!

954
01:19:28,213 --> 01:19:30,012
- Thanks.
- What did you get there?

955
01:19:35,055 --> 01:19:36,445
Ta-daa!

956
01:19:37,341 --> 01:19:39,327
[LAUGHING]

957
01:19:42,469 --> 01:19:44,482
Turn around, smile!

958
01:19:45,589 --> 01:19:49,428
- Oh, look at you!
- Wonderful!

959
01:19:49,452 --> 01:19:50,560
Oh, my!

960
01:19:54,399 --> 01:19:55,773
- Give us a smile!

961
01:20:01,084 --> 01:20:03,417
- Bye-bye!
- Bye!

962
01:20:03,441 --> 01:20:04,876
Bye, Beanie!

963
01:20:07,110 --> 01:20:08,393
- See ya!

964
01:20:12,675 --> 01:20:14,520
- Farewell!

965
01:20:24,402 --> 01:20:25,935
Well, Beanie...

966
01:20:26,861 --> 01:20:30,509
it's been...
[CHUCKLES] surreal.

967
01:20:31,395 --> 01:20:35,299
Uh.. I want you to know you
can come back and visit anytime.

968
01:20:36,643 --> 01:20:39,314
In the fairly distant future.
[CHUCKLES]

969
01:20:40,725 --> 01:20:42,683
[CHUCKLING]

970
01:20:43,598 --> 01:20:48,040
I- I know it sounds insane,
but I'm really gonna miss you.

971
01:20:51,244 --> 01:20:52,613
Okay.

972
01:20:54,073 --> 01:20:55,362
Okay.

973
01:21:03,289 --> 01:21:05,032
[EXCLAIMS]

974
01:21:07,900 --> 01:21:09,046
Oh!

975
01:21:12,029 --> 01:21:14,555
Okay. Goodbye again.

976
01:21:18,733 --> 01:21:22,394
[SIGHS, SNORTS]

977
01:21:56,517 --> 01:21:57,622
[SNAPS FINGERS]

978
01:22:53,001 --> 01:22:57,099
[BOYZONE'S "PICTURE
OF YOU" PLAYS]

979
01:24:46,222 --> 01:24:52,280
[THOMAS JULES' "THAT
KINDA GUY" PLAYS]

980
01:26:37,181 --> 01:26:42,183
[ALISHA'S ATTIC'S
"HE'S A REBEL" PLAYS]

981
01:28:44,597 --> 01:28:47,506
Yes, I normally stay
'til the end as well.

982
01:28:50,115 --> 01:28:51,476
Bye.

983
01:28:58,799 --> 01:29:02,275
You can go now if you...
If you wish.

984
01:29:05,622 --> 01:29:06,964
Bye.

